<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Yabghus of Tokharistan</h1><p class="st-meta">625 CE – 758 CE (approx.) · capital Kunduz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkic dynasty that ruled Tokharistan, the old Bactria, from Kunduz, holding together a patchwork of principalities between the Turks, Tang China and the Arabs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Bactria</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tong-yabghu-khagan">Tong Yabghu Khagan</a> installed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tardu-shad">Tardu Shad</a> as yabghu, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> around 625, with his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> met Tardu Shad there shortly before he was poisoned.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The yabghus were overlords of some 27 local principalities, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-khuttal">Khuttal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan">Chaghaniyan</a> and Bamiyan, many ruled by Hephthalite dynasties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished, with great monasteries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and Bamiyan. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> system the yabghus received Chinese titles and sent embassies asking for help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, who took Balkh and pressed into the region from the 650s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The yabghus became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, and in 709 to 710 the Hephthalite prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizak-tarkhan">Nizak Tarkhan</a>, who held the yabghu in his custody, led a revolt crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a>. The dynasty survived as Abbasid clients, with records of envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until about 758, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> was absorbed into Islamic Khurasan.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yabghus-of-tokharistan.jpg" alt="Yabghus of Tokharistan" width="320" height="219"><figcaption>Image: पाटलिपुत्र · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokhara_Yabghus_tamgha.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>625 CE – 758 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kunduz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tardu Shad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bactrian, Old Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Afghanistan, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tokharistan Yabghus, Yabghu of Tokharistan</dd></dl></aside></div>

